INT. WIRELESS ROOM - S.S. CALYPSO - NIGHT, 1923

A cramped brass-and-mahogany cabin lit by a green-shaded lamp. The telegraph key chatters. Beyond the porthole, a black sea.

LADY PHILIPPA ASHGROVE, fifties, in a fur-collared evening gown, waits with gloved hands folded. At the desk, young AMBROSE TILLERY, the wireless operator, copies a message with a pencil. Behind her, MR. CORNELIUS BLY, sixty, a banker with a white moustache, stands very still. In the corner sits MADAME ZORKA DRAGOMIR, a fortune-teller with a velvet shawl, humming softly.

AMBROSE
That's the last of it, my lady. "Meet at Southampton. Say nothing."

PHILIPPA
Thank you, Ambrose. Cornelius, you may relax.

BLY
I am perfectly relaxed.

PHILIPPA
Of course you are.

ZORKA
(smiling)
The sea is very calm tonight.

The door opens. INSPECTOR HALVARD STROM, forties, in a damp greatcoat and bowler, steps inside, a pair of handcuffs dangling from one hand.

STROM
Mr. Cornelius Bly. I have a warrant from Scotland Yard.

Bly turns. Philippa's gloved hands do not move. Madame Zorka stops humming.

PHILIPPA
I did say nothing.

BLY
You said nothing was the plan.

AMBROSE
(whispering)
Oh, dear.
